        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did José Blanco F. attend?\n\nAnswer A: Aristotle University of Thessaloniki\n  about: José Blanco F.\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1985-1989\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Blanco F. attended the Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, a prestigious higher education institution based in Thessaloniki, Greece, from 1985 to 1989.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Florida State University\n  about: José Blanco F.\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to credible sources, Blanco F. attended Florida State University, a public university located in Tallahassee, Florida, United States.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: José Blanco F.\n\nJosé Blanco F. is a Dominican University professor known for his contributions to the field of education. Born on February 12, 1965, in Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic, Blanco F. has had a distinguished academic career, with a significant portion of it spent at the Aristotle University of Thessaloniki. Early Life and Education\n\nBlanco F. was raised in a family of educators and was encouraged from a young age to pursue higher education. He completed his primary and secondary education in the Dominican Republic before moving to Greece to pursue his undergraduate degree. Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (1985-1989)\n\nBlanco F. attended the Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, a prestigious higher education institution based in Thessaloniki, Greece, from 1985 to 1989. During his time at the university, he was enrolled in the Faculty of Philosophy, where he studied philosophy, pedagogy, and psychology. According to a study published in the Journal of Educational Research and Development, Blanco F.'s time at Aristotle University of Thessaloniki had a profound impact on his academic and professional development. The study, titled \"The Impact of International Education on Academic Achievement,\" found that students who studied abroad, like Blanco F., demonstrated higher levels of academic achievement and cultural competence compared to their peers who did not have international educational experiences. [1]\n\nAcademic Career\n\nAfter completing his undergraduate degree, Blanco F. returned to the Dominican Republic, where he began his academic career as a professor at the Dominican University. He has since become a renowned expert in the field of education, publishing numerous papers and books on topics such as educational psychology, curriculum development, and teacher training. References\n\n[1] Papadopoulos, G., & Georgiou, S. (2005). The Impact of International Education on Academic Achievement. Journal of Educational Research and Development, 1(1), 12-25. [2] Blanco F., J. (1995). The Role of Philosophy in Education. Journal of Educational Philosophy, 17(2), 34-45. [3] Aristotle University of Thessaloniki. (n.d.). Faculty of Philosophy. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.auth.gr\\/en\\/faculties\\/philosophy>\n\n[4] Dominican University. (n.d.).\nDocument 2: **José Blanco F.**\n\nJosé Blanco F. is a Dominican university professor who has made significant contributions to the field of education in the Dominican Republic. **Early Life and Education**\n\nJosé Blanco F. was born in the Dominican Republic and pursued higher education in the United States. According to credible sources, Blanco F. attended Florida State University, a public university located in Tallahassee, Florida, United States. **Attendance at Florida State University**\n\nBlanco F.'s attendance at Florida State University is supported by multiple sources. In an interview with Listín Diario, a prominent Dominican newspaper, Blanco F. himself mentioned that he studied at Florida State University. [1] Additionally, a biography published by the Dominican Ministry of Higher Education, Science, and Technology also confirms that Blanco F. attended Florida State University. [2]\n\nFlorida State University is a reputable institution that has produced many notable alumni, and Blanco F.'s attendance at the university is a testament to his commitment to academic excellence. **References**\n\n[1] Listín Diario. (2018). Entrevista a José Blanco F. [Interview with José Blanco F.]. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.listindiario.com\\/la-republica\\/2018\\/02\\/20\\/entrevista-a-jose-blanco-f\\/>\n\n[2] Ministry of Higher Education, Science, and Technology of the Dominican Republic. (2020). Biografía de José Blanco F. [Biography of José Blanco F.].\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
